    <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Dead Pixel vs Stuck Pixel</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>dead pixel</strong> stays permanently black on every color — it isn't
      receiving any power. A <strong>stuck pixel</strong> stays fixed on one color
      (often red, green, or blue) because a single sub-pixel is stuck on. Run the
      black, white, red, green, and blue screens above one at a time — a dead pixel
      shows as a dark dot on white/color screens; a stuck pixel shows as a colored
      dot on the black screen.
    </p>

    <div class="info-grid">
      <div class="info-card">
        <div class="info-card-icon">⚫</div>
        <div class="info-card-title">Backlight Bleed</div>
        <div class="info-card-desc">
          Run the black screen in a dark room at full brightness. Look for
          glowing patches near the edges or corners.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <div class="info-card-icon">🌈</div>
        <div class="info-card-title">Color Banding — 6-bit vs 8-bit</div>
        <div class="info-card-desc">
          The banding ramp reveals visible steps instead of a smooth gradient.
          Cheaper laptop panels are often true 6-bit (or 6-bit + FRC dithering)
          rather than true 8-bit — this shows up as harder edges in the
          gradient, especially in dark grays. Heavy banding here often means a
          lower bit-depth panel or GPU dithering turned off.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <div class="info-card-icon">📐</div>
        <div class="info-card-title">Screen Uniformity</div>
        <div class="info-card-desc">
          The 50% gray screen reveals patchy brightness or a color tint across
          the panel that's invisible on regular content.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <div class="info-card-icon">🎞️</div>
        <div class="info-card-title">Motion Blur</div>
        <div class="info-card-desc">
          Watch the moving blocks without following them with your eyes —
          trailing/ghosting is more visible on slower-response panels.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <div class="info-card-icon">📐</div>
        <div class="info-card-title">Viewing Angle Shift</div>
        <div class="info-card-desc">
          TN panels shift color and contrast sharply off-angle; IPS panels stay
          stable. Tilt your screen slightly while on the Uniformity test to
          check which type you have.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Checking for PWM Flicker</h2>
    <p>
      Browsers cannot detect PWM (Pulse-Width Modulation) flicker directly — it
      happens at the hardware backlight level, faster than any web API can
      measure. But you can check for it yourself in under a minute:
    </p>
    <div class="info-grid">
      <div class="info-card">
        <div class="info-card-icon">📱</div>
        <div class="info-card-title">Camera Slow-Motion Method</div>
        <div class="info-card-desc">
          Open your phone camera, switch to slow-motion video mode, set this
          tool's brightness swatch to Gray, dim your laptop brightness to around
          30–50%, and film the screen. Horizontal dark bands scrolling through
          the recording indicate PWM flicker.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <div class="info-card-icon">✋</div>
        <div class="info-card-title">Quick Wave Test</div>
        <div class="info-card-desc">
          With the Gray swatch open, wave a pen or your finger quickly in front
          of the screen at low brightness. A smooth blur means no visible
          flicker; a stuttering, strobing trail suggests PWM at low brightness
          levels.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <div class="info-card-icon">👁️</div>
        <div class="info-card-title">Why It Matters</div>
        <div class="info-card-desc">
          PWM flicker is usually invisible to the naked eye but can contribute
          to eye strain or headaches for sensitive users, especially at lower
          brightness settings where flicker frequency often drops.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">What is a dead pixel vs a stuck pixel?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">
          A dead pixel stays permanently black — it receives no power at all. A
          stuck pixel stays fixed on one color (usually red, green, or blue)
          because one sub-pixel is stuck on while others are off. Stuck pixels
          can sometimes be fixed with pressure or flickering methods; dead
          pixels usually cannot.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Can a browser detect dead pixels automatically?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">
          No. A browser has no way to read back what your screen is physically
          displaying — it can only show you solid colors to look at. Detecting a
          dead or stuck pixel still requires your eyes; this tool's job is to
          give you clean, full-screen reference colors to check against.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">
          What is backlight bleed and how do I check for it?
        </div>
        <div class="faq-a">
          Backlight bleed is light leaking around the edges or corners of an LCD
          panel, most visible on a full black screen in a dark room. Run the
          black screen test, dim your room lights, and look for uneven glowing
          patches near the bezel.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">
          Why does my screen look slightly different in this test than in real
          use?
        </div>
        <div class="faq-a">
          Browser color rendering depends on your display's color profile, OS
          color management, and any blue-light/night-mode filters running. For
          the most accurate test, disable night light mode and run this test at
          100% brightness in a dim room.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">
          Is this the same as a hardware dead pixel checker?
        </div>
        <div class="faq-a">
          It follows the same principle as dedicated hardware checkers —
          full-screen solid colors — but runs entirely in your browser with no
          download. For panels you suspect have an issue, testing in a dark room
          at full brightness gives the most reliable result.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
